Every book should be a recognizable physical object.

Create several carefully designed base models and material combinations that can represent many individual books without generating an unnecessarily complex new 3D mesh for every creation.

Allow customization of:

- Front-cover material.
- Cover colour.
- Leather, fabric, or hardbound appearance.
- Embossed or engraved decorative details.
- Title placement.
- Spine title.
- Gold, brass, or restrained silver accents.
- Page colour.
- Ruling lines.
- Page margins.
- Book thickness as the document grows.
- Optional cover illustration.

The physical object should correspond to the user's actual book.

A thick manuscript should look more substantial than a newly created notebook. A book with saved entries should visually retain its identity when the user closes and reopens the application.

Make book titles readable on the spine where the available geometry permits. For narrow books, display a shorter title and show the complete name on selection.

### Book interaction

On hover, gently highlight the selected book and move it forward slightly.

On selection, bring it toward the camera and reveal its title, category, and available actions.

Clicking “OPEN” should trigger a cinematic transition in which the cover opens around the spine hinge and the pages become visible.

Clicking the spine or a designated book-control area should never accidentally delete or modify the document.

Provide clear actions for opening, renaming, moving, duplicating, and archiving books.

Deleting a book must require confirmation.

## 8. THE ANTIQUE MECHANICAL TYPEWRITER

Create an exceptional, highly detailed, three-dimensional mechanical typewriter located on the writing desk.

It should have a dark burgundy or black enamelled body, aged brass trim, a full set of individually animated round keys, a realistic cylindrical platen, a carriage, paper guides, a paper bail, and a believable typebar assembly.

Use premium materials, intricate mechanical details, and physically plausible proportions.

The machine should be the recognizable signature object of TYPEWRITER.

Each key should have a clearly visible letter or character legend. The keys must respond to actual keyboard input when the user is typing.

When a key is pressed:

1. Animate the corresponding visual key downward.
2. Move the corresponding typebar or representative striking mechanism toward the page.
3. Show a brief, believable contact action.
4. Reveal the actual typed character on the paper.
5. Advance the carriage appropriately.
6. Return the key and mechanism to their resting positions.

The response must be immediate enough for comfortable typing.

At the end of a line, animate a smooth carriage return, advance the paper by one line, and resume writing.

Use subtle mechanical sounds when enabled. Include independent sound controls and ensure that the default audio experience is not intrusive.

Support ordinary keyboard input, punctuation, spaces, newlines, editing, and corrections.

The typewriter should not be an animation that merely plays independently. It must be synchronized with the actual text entered by the user.

## 9. THE LIVE PAPER-WRITING EXPERIENCE

When a book is opened for writing, transition into an interactive workspace centered on the typewriter and its paper.

The user must be able to type using their real computer keyboard.

Create a genuine editable text input as the underlying source of truth, and display the same text accurately on the three-dimensional paper.

The writing surface should have a light, aged-parchment texture with crisp, readable dark ink.

For diary and journal templates, include delicate horizontal lines and a subtle vertical margin. For research reports and manuscripts, use a more suitable document layout while preserving the overall aesthetic.

Keep the text within the page margins. Use sensible line wrapping and line spacing. Ensure that the visible text remains aligned with its actual document content.

If the paper is displayed at an angle, align a transparent HTML or equivalent rendering layer with the page using the correct perspective transformation. The text should look as though it belongs to the paper rather than floating in front of it.

Do not bake user-generated text into permanent texture maps.

The user must be able to select, correct, replace, edit, and delete text using normal editing controls.

Provide a clear way to switch between immersive typewriter mode and a comfortable, more traditional editing mode for situations where long-form editing requires precise cursor placement, selection, formatting, or accessibility support.

The visual mode should remain beautifully immersive, but functionality must take priority over animation.

## 10. PAPER COMPLETION AND BOOK-BINDING ANIMATION

Create the signature transition that connects typing to preserving a finished page.

When the user fills a sheet, stop at the appropriate page boundary and show a subtle completion state.

Do not automatically archive the page before the content has been saved successfully.

Offer:

“PRESERVE THIS PAGE”

“CONTINUE WRITING”

When the user chooses to preserve the page:

- The typewriter finishes its current line.
- The carriage returns smoothly.
- The paper-release mechanism opens.
- The completed sheet slides gently out of the platen.
- The sheet retains the exact typed content.
- The camera follows the paper toward the selected book.
- The book opens to the correct destination.
- The sheet aligns with the intended archive page.
- The page settles into the book with a believable paper-bending animation.
- A subtle confirmation indicates that the page has been preserved successfully.

The visual metaphor should suggest that the user's words have become a permanent page in their personal book.

For diary and journal entries, treat the sheet as a preserved page of the document. For a research report or manuscript, insert the page into the relevant section of that document.

Keep the underlying document content and its structure intact throughout the animation.

The paper-transfer effect is a visual representation of a completed writing operation—not the actual mechanism of data storage. Save the page to the application first, confirm success, and then perform the celebratory animation.

If the user chooses to continue writing, load a fresh sheet or transition into the next page without losing the previous content.

Make the animation skippable. Never force the user to wait through a long sequence after every page.

## 11. THE BOOK EDITOR AND PAGE SYSTEM

The inside of every book should be a fully functional editing environment.

When the book opens, the user should see a beautifully modelled page or spread.

Support real document editing, including:

- Editing existing entries.
- Creating new pages.
- Renaming entries.
- Editing dates and titles.
- Moving between pages.
- Adding and organizing chapters.
- Adding headings and subheadings.
- Applying appropriate formatting for the selected book type.
- Inserting images where supported.
- Creating tables or lists where appropriate.
- Searching within a book.
- Reordering relevant sections.
- Saving and retrieving changes.

Diary entries should support multiple independently editable entries. A long-form book should support chapters and sections rather than treating the entire manuscript as a single diary entry.

A research report should support structured sections and a navigable contents panel.

All content must be stored independently from the 3D visuals so that a user can change a cover, page texture, or animation without affecting their writing.

### Page navigation

Allow page turns using elegant previous/next controls, page-edge interactions where reliable, and direct navigation through the document's index.

Animate page turns with natural curvature and smooth easing.

Avoid unnecessary long transitions during normal navigation.

If the user is editing text, prevent accidental page turns or loss of unsaved input.

